* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files need CRLF line endings to run reliably. `-text` turns
# off line-ending conversion for these files, so `git add` keeps their CRLF
# bytes in the blob. Under `text`, with any `eol` value, git would store LF.
# To show it, run this command in a clone. It stores CRLF bytes as a commit
# of start_windows.bat would, then prints the stored blob:
#   printf 'a\r\nb\r\n' | git hash-object -w --stdin --path=start_windows.bat | xargs git cat-file -p | od -c
# Under `-text` the output shows a `\r` before each `\n`. Under `text`,
# `text eol=crlf`, or `text eol=lf` it shows no `\r`.
# scripts/tests/test_launcher_line_endings.sh checks every line break in each
# blob.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# Shell/command launchers must stay LF
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
